  "body": "https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsers.html#windowproxy-delete\r\n> If P is an array index property name, then return false\\.\r\n\r\nIIUC, this statement says that it must return `false` as long as `P` is an array index property name regardless of the actual number of child browsing contexts.  For example,\r\n```javascript\r\nwindow.length  // => 1\r\ndelete window[0]  // => false, because it does not remove the child browsing context.\r\ndelete window[3]  // => false??\r\n```\r\nHowever, [ECMAScript](https://tc39.github.io/ecma262/#table-5) seems expecting that it returns `true` because there is no such property, i.e. `Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(window, 3)` returns `undefined`.\r\n\r\nhttps://tc39.github.io/ecma262/#table-5\r\n> [[Delete]]\r\n> (propertyKey) → Boolean\r\n> Remove the own property whose key is propertyKey from this object. Return false if the property was not deleted and is still present. Return true if the property was deleted or is not present.\r\n\r\nBy the way, Firefox(Gecko) returns `true` in such a case, and I'm now wondering what I should implement in Chrome(Blink).\r\n",
